f1d518d0425d7373280820bba28aa6725332b90690ad273"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-8","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":8,"english":"The lord of Ayodhyā, on hearing the words of that wicked-minded one, Anaranya, enraged, addressed the Raksasa-chief, saying, 'O king of Raksasas, I will give you combat, stay you. At once prepare for fight, and I also shall go and prepare myself.'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.886,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-19-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7019008","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"af06d024192ed535e748fa74dc3867cbdf7bbb267a81c7eabfaa0bf54dff56c3","compact":"c48156e88ccb62b4cd77f4f2453edff9c9d99a87e3ef9dfa14ded4df11eb04ff","nasal":"2d34c8faf28db8c238fd75256c1610443d60de72b54fb66d7a9e08227e05b75b"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-9","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":9,"english":"When he had heard everything (regarding Răvaņa), the forces of that foremost of kings that had been intended for conquering (Ravana), sallied forth ready for bringing about the destruction of the of the Raksasa, ten-thousand elephants, a niyuta horse, and many thousands of cars and infantry, O best of men; and, that host consisting of infantry and cars, marched for encounter, covering up the earth.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.625,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-19-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7019009","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cf49e8f322f665ec5cefd6bd97fecfbe1f8d4ca138309de2f71c6af33b244345","compact":"44896d20925256f48faf4dd932e7dc938de81a94a2e0610e4738f933a243407f","nasal":"cdeb6a59d91d95be9c89fe255707f6c50e425ce123ed409f53b7acebd276568d"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-10","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":10,"english":"When he had heard everything (regarding Răvaņa), the forces of that foremost of kings that had been intended for conquering (Ravana), sallied forth ready for bringing about the destruction of the of the Raksasa, ten-thousand elephants, a niyuta horse, and many thousands of cars and infantry, O best of men; and, that host consisting of infantry and cars, marched for encounter, covering up the earth.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.619,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-19-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7019010","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"43ba2ec12019ea3b93251df761fc453bcbd356223bfe5bd09e89beb92659c6f1","compact":"44f35c67b454d61f17500842b6f7d6a31b91c1a37c5974e0fa6223f24833e7d9","nasal":"e375c49333410b6c65f7ef87d4e45cced71810f4213b9ab3100c5c3c3e6013f0"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-11","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":11,"english":"That host, of the king encountering the forces of Rāvana, were extinguished like to clarified butter thrown into the (sacrificial) fire. And having fought valorously for a long time, the remnant of the royal forces, suddenly coming in contact with the flaming Räkşasas ranks, were destroyed like to swarms of locusts entering into fire.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.786,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-19-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7019011","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"15d3a8c24e424bc3c553237372c9fce8edbdc135e1abf7d38e7377f2db8ba244","compact":"7ee9b31feadab9faae530ae376912ec7055280144ef54b516a5a735d19f578c4","nasal":"7ee9b31feadab9faae530ae376912ec7055280144ef54b516a5a735d19f578c4"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-12","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":12,"english":"He saw the mighty army of that powerful monarch destroyed by the (adversary), like to a hundred streams absorbed by an approaching ocean.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.475,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-19-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7019012","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f5a47186ddb553455a0ffa43762b2877f5b75240f57e729f8d20c2ca3a03ceb3","compact":"78a0db4ae9383bee730682f347cf6e92cca9b75d2b2869e42c07dd62dfc4079a","nasal":"fe9cfe1784b6086c2b5d3a65561ed0a800374fd9fc2eb3ab29401934298cb9f6"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-13","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":13,"english":"And then himself drawing his bow resembling the bow itself of Sakra, that foremost of sovereigns, beside himself with wrath, approached Rāvana.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.765,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-19-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7019013","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"eeaa646217a2eb25baffc021ecda97825735136dde7d1f447fd8e0153f4aabed","compact":"cd029eef9960bc2bb8860b955210e083402b857a209d71483e648663821ab745","nasal":"ad7384b456369c5785cc6a4f13d6577dab262ea9380c069dd4a49ab25d66db63"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-14","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":14,"english":"Then that son of the Ikşvāku race discharged eight hundred arrows at Rāvana's head.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-19-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7019014","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b562277f7694e243bb9811e0af1166986bc3d5b76b7d69338501888639ca2e0d","compact":"a420663758629618986776349f1594c65b274592c280d1ef0ff4d2d6f99a0fc5","nasal":"3c67a4d1d9630cbad3aa7a637acc457e48a5831fea49aad7b341ddee12f3a77c"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-19-15","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":19,"verse":15,"english":"And like to showers pouring down on the top of a mountain, his shafts did not inflict any wound (on Ravana).","alignm